    "id": "prop:common-baseline-reduction",
    "kind": "proposition",
    "statement": "Sanity check: When \\(P(X=0)=1\\) and \\(Z>0\\) almost surely, the obstacle program collapses to the scalar interval for the common baseline trend and reproduces the bounded-conditional-average-slope WAOSS bounds of de Chaisemartin, Ciccia, D'Haultf\\oe{}uille, and Knau (2024).",
    "free_symbols": [
      "P",
      "X",
      "Z"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "thm:obstacle-compatibility",
      "thm:attained-interval"
    ],
    "justification": "This reduction is the required sanity check and fixes the boundary between prior art and the varying-baseline contribution.",
    "gap": "The scalar common-zero-baseline result is published; the proposition claims equivalence, not novelty.",
    "consumer": "Readers can verify that existing heterogeneous-adoption bounds are nested exactly within the new frontier.",
    "proof_tex": "Suppose \\(P(X=0)=1\\) and \\(Z>0\\) almost surely.  Then \\(S=1\\), \\(X_0=\\{0\\}\\), and\n\\[\n \\nu_P=\\delta_0.\n\\]\nA continuous baseline trend is a single scalar \\(c=u(0)\\); there is no nontrivial cross-baseline Lipschitz restriction.  The obstacle definitions reduce to\n\\[\n \\underline c\n =\\max\\left\\{-M,\\sup_{(0,z)\\in H}\\{g(0,z)-Kz\\}\\right\\},\n \\qquad\n \\overline c\n =\\min\\left\\{M,\\inf_{(0,z)\\in H}\\{g(0,z)+Kz\\}\\right\\}.\n\\]\nIndeed, \\(c\\in[\\underline c,\\overline c]\\) is equivalent term by term to\n\\[\n |c|\\leq M,\n \\qquad\n |g(0,z)-c|\\leq Kz\n \\quad((0,z)\\in H).\n\\]\nThus \\(\\text{thm:obstacle-compatibility}\\) gives\n\\[\n \\mathcal C_P=\\{c\\in\\mathbb R:\\underline c\\leq c\\leq\\overline c\\},\n\\]\nwith emptiness exactly when \\(\\underline c>\\overline c\\).  Under compatibility the endpoints are ordered.  Since \\(J_P(c)=c\\), \\(\\text{thm:attained-interval}\\) becomes\n\\[\n \\Theta(P)=\n \\left[\\frac{T-\\overline c}{A},\n       \\frac{T-\\underline c}{A}\\right].\n\\]\nThis is exactly the scalar intersection program for the common-zero-baseline bounded conditional-average-slope model in de Chaisemartin, Ciccia, D'Haultf\\oe{}uille, and Knau (2024, arXiv:2405.04465v4, Section 4.2.1, Theorem 4, and Appendix B.2).  The argument establishes algebraic recovery of that prior-art interval and makes no novelty claim for the scalar case.",
